### Design Patterns

- Event Sourcing (not event driven):
  Using events to trace the history of our application state.
  Instead of persisting the new state at every change, we persist only the change, specifying the 	business reason - the business event. Give us an audit flow of who did what on entity.
  This way => reconstruct the history of the entity up to the current state, calculate the state 	of any time in the past, only need to process all the events up to a certain time.
  Can use the stream of events to collect other statistics that can be hard to obtain otherwise.
  Benefits: The ability to:
  1. Maintain the information about the business event behind the change
  2. Collect all the audit (kiem toan, su xet) data
  3. Reconstruct the state a a certain time in the past
  4. Create multi-different views of the same data.

- Application Facade:
  As a facade or proxy for a complex system, hiding the more
  complex innards and making it easy to interact with.
  We want to use it to extract info and give commands to our Application without
  cluttering the test with technical details (an interface that abstracts
  the interactions with our helper, allowing both querying its state and changing it).


